 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Max Depth Cap**: At the maximum octree depth, the algorithm does not traverse further into children but instead calls `traverse_cap`, implying leaf nodes at max depth represent cap geometry rather than volumetric data [S4].
-*   **Transfer Function Changes**: When the transfer function changes, the min/max ranges are used to reclassify regions. Empty Adaptive Block Refinement (ABR) regions can be deactivated, requiring an interactive rebuild of the BVH if used in conjunction [S4].
-*   **Precision**: The min/max pruning relies on the scalar range. If the isovalue is exactly at the boundary, the node is traversed, which is safe but may reduce pruning efficiency [S4].
+*   **Max Depth Cap**: At the maximum octree depth, the algorithm does not traverse further into children but instead calls `traverse_cap`, implying leaf nodes at max depth represent cap geometry rather than volumetric data [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Transfer Function Changes**: When the transfer function changes, the min/max ranges are used to reclassify regions. Empty Adaptive Block Refinement (ABR) regions can be deactivated, requiring an interactive rebuild of the BVH if used in conjunction [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Precision**: The min/max pruning relies on the scalar range. Boundary cases where the isovalue exactly matches a stored min or max value are handled by the inclusive range check, ensuring safe traversal at the cost of potentially reduced pruning efficiency [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 
 ### Time and Memory Complexity
-*   **Memory**: The octree stores min/max values for each node, adding overhead to the scalar data. The structure is lossless compressed, occupying a fraction of the original memory footprint [S2, S3].
-*   **Time**: Traversal is front-to-back, allowing early termination. Pruning efficiency depends on the variance of scalar values within nodes. High variance leads to more traversal; low variance leads to more skipping [S1, S4].
